From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 02:05:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] ios: make the render target the size the screen actually is

The 6.1.3 log carries the whole bug, in two lines nothing reconciles:

  [gles-angle] frame buffer on an IOSurface (320x480, 1280 bytes a row)
  [gles] present target -> base=0x07b0f000 960x640 fmt=1111970369

The host renders into 320x480. CoreAnimation presents 960x640. And
320x480 is not a measurement of anything -- it is the initialiser of
gles_fb_w/h, which gles_host_init corrected from exactly one machine:

    object_dynamic_cast(OBJECT(qdev_get_machine()), TYPE_IPOD_TOUCH_MACHINE)

So the iPod touch 2G got its real panel, the 3GS kept 320x480 and was
right by coincidence, and every A4 board -- iPhone 4, iPod touch 4G,
iPad 1, which is every board that runs iOS 6 -- kept it and was wrong.
That the one configuration reported as working is iOS 3 on the 3GS is
not a coincidence either.

What that did to a frame, in order:

  * The app asks its drawable how big it is. glGetRenderbufferParameteriv
    answers out of gh.drawable_width, so it is told 320x480, and it sizes
    its viewport to that and draws the whole scene into a quarter of the
    screen.
  * The present then crops to the same guess:
        rw = MIN(width, gh.drawable_width)     MIN(960, 320) = 320
        rh = MIN(height, gh.drawable_height)   MIN(640, 480) = 480
    320x480 of pixels into a 960x640 surface.

Rendered correctly, presented into the corner -- which is what Need for
Speed Shift looks like, and the log agrees frame by frame: ok=1 failed=0,
nothing refused, nothing dropped. A game that instead checks its
framebuffer and finds the host's 320x480 attachments under its own
960x640 renderbuffer is the crash on open.

Three changes, each from a number the guest or the machine already tells
us rather than from a default:

  * gles_host_init takes the panel's size from the display's own graphic
    console. Every board registers one and resizes it in its realize
    (ipod_touch_lcd.c, s5l8930_display.c), both from the panel numbers the
    panel= override feeds, so it is authoritative before any guest code
    runs and it needs no machine type. The old cast stays as the fallback.
    The size is now printed, which no log before this named.
  * gles_present_to_surface adopts the size CoreAnimation is presenting
    into, so the copy is exact and is re-adopted on rotation. An app on
    iOS 6 never sends GLES_OP_DRAWABLE_STORAGE at all, so this is the only
    place the real size is ever stated. It happens before the size check,
    which would otherwise refuse the new size the one time it mattered.
  * gles_angle_iosurface_bind and gles_eagl_iosurface_bind took a width
    and a height and ignored them once a surface existed -- one frame
    buffer per process, made at init, never resized -- so a resized
    drawable either kept the old surface or, on EAGL, was told "bound" and
    got an attachment of the wrong size. Both remember the size they were
    made at and rebuild for a new one, and gles_drawable_storage attaches
    at the new size instead of setting gh.iosurface = false and falling
    back to readback. It keeps the retreat to readback for the case
    gles_host_init already handles: a driver that takes an IOSurface and
    then will not render to it.

The last of those also closes a silent one. Readback presents through
gles_present_to_panel, which needs nms->lcd_state and so returned, with
nothing in the log, on every board that is not the iPod touch: a frame
rendered and then presented nowhere. Both of its returns say why now,
once each.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UGCtLRD1vYicb9wVRzGR7X
